---
import Package from "../layouts/Package.astro";
import { href } from "../lib";
---

<Package
  slug="secure-delivery/"
  title="Secure delivery setup"
  description="Hardened, reusable pipelines, protection rules and short-lived cloud access, built in your own GitHub organisation or GitLab group and handed over for your team to own."
  lead="Pipelines that are safe by default and easy for every team to use, built in your own GitHub or GitLab and handed over. You own all of it, with no licence and no support contract."
  forWho="Teams on GitHub or GitLab that deploy to AWS"
>
  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <ul class="ticks">
    <li>Teams whose pipelines have grown repository by repository, each a little different.</li>
    <li>Teams that hold long-lived cloud keys in CI and want rid of them.</li>
    <li>Teams that need to show customers or auditors how their software is built and released.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is included</h2>
  <ul class="ticks">
    <li>Shared, reusable pipelines for build, test, security scanning and deployment, which your projects call instead of copying: reusable workflows on GitHub, CI/CD components on GitLab.</li>
    <li>Protection for your main branches and releases (rulesets on GitHub, protected branches and merge request approvals on GitLab), defined as code, with a check that reports if anyone weakens them.</li>
    <li>Automatic, reviewed updates for dependencies and pipeline components.</li>
    <li>Short-lived access from pipelines to AWS through OIDC, with each pipeline's access scoped to exactly what it needs, replacing stored keys.</li>
    <li>Signed builds, so you can show exactly where a release came from, where you need it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every change arrives as a pull or merge request in your repositories, so your team reviews each piece as
    it lands. <a href={href("how-we-build/")}>See how we run the same setup ourselves</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What you receive</h2>
  <ul class="ticks">
    <li>Working pipelines in your repositories, and the rules that protect them.</li>
    <li>Written documentation of how it fits together and how to change it.</li>
    <li>A handover session with your engineers, who will already have used and changed it during the work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is not included</h2>
  <ul class="excludes">
    <li>Running or supporting the pipelines afterwards. Your team owns them from the handover.</li>
    <li>Changes to your application code.</li>
    <li>Building new cloud environments or Kubernetes clusters.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On GitHub, if you use our public workflows, later improvements reach you as versioned releases, offered
    as pull requests your team chooses whether to merge. On GitLab, the versioned components live in your
    own group, and are yours to update.
  </p>
</Package>
